RHEL 7 and later versions includes a new tool systemd-tmpfiles, which provides a structured and configurable method to manage temporary directories and files

In Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL), systemd-tmpfiles is the core utility used to manage temporary files and volatile directories. It handles their creation, owner/permission assignments, and periodic cleanup automatically, ensuring the system filesystem does not run out of space.


⚙️ How it Works Under the Hood

The systemd-tmpfiles utility operates through specific systemd services and timers:
• On Boot (systemd-tmpfiles-setup.service): Runs systemd-tmpfiles --create --remove to initialize required system volatile directories and files.
• Scheduled Cleanup (systemd-tmpfiles-clean.timer): A daily timer that triggers systemd-tmpfiles-clean.service to execute systemd-tmpfiles --clean. This parses your age-based rules and deletes expired data.


📂 Configuration File Locations

systemd-tmpfiles reads configuration files ending in .conf. Never edit files in /usr/lib/ directly, as updates will overwrite them. Instead, use /etc/tmpfiles.d/ for your custom entries:

Path	Purpose	    Priority
/etc/tmpfiles.d/*.conf	Administrator custom rules (Use this)	Highest (Overrides distribution defaults)
/run/tmpfiles.d/*.conf	Runtime configurations created dynamically	Medium
/usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/*.conf	Distribution-provided rules (packaged by vendors)	Lowest



Lowest


📝 Configuration File Syntax

Rules inside a .conf file use the following structured format:
# Type  Path                    Mode  User  Group  Age  Argument
d       /run/myapp/locks        0755  root  root   -    -
d       /var/cache/myapp/tmp    0700  appuser appuser 7d   -


Key Fields Breakdown:

1. Type (Action): Tells systemd what to do with the target path.
	• d: Create a directory if it doesn't exist, and clean up its contents based on the Age field.
	• D: Create or empty a directory, and remove all contents during boot/cleanup.
	• e: Clean up an existing directory's contents based on age (does not create the directory).
	• f: Create a file if it doesn't exist.
	• x: Exclude a path from cleanup rules. (Handy for protecting specific files in /tmp).
2. Path: The absolute path to the directory or file.
3. Mode: File permissions (e.g., 0755 or 0700). Use - to inherit or leave defaults.
4. User & Group: The owner and group ownership assignments.
5. Age: The expiration threshold before a file is targeted for deletion.
	• Supported suffixes: s (seconds), m (minutes), h (hours), d (days), w (weeks).
	• Use - if you never want the system to automatically purge this path.

🚀 Step-by-Step Implementation


Step 1: Create a Custom Rule File

Create a new file under /etc/tmpfiles.d/. For example, to manage a custom application scratch space:
sudo vi /etc/tmpfiles.d/custom-app.conf

Add a rule to manage a directory that deletes files older than 5 days:
# Type  Path               Mode  User     Group    Age  Argument
d       /var/tmp/custom-app 0755  appuser  appuser  5d   -

